Rain City by Ilia Reingold (ShaderDesk)
https://github.com/ilrein/shaderdesk/blob/main/Scenes/RainCity.metal
(file last changed in commit 94c77888, "Rain City: rainy cyberpunk skyline with neon signs,
searchlights and flying cars", 2026-09-29)

The shader file itself carries no licence notice. It was written for the ShaderDesk
repository, whose licence covers it:

  https://github.com/ilrein/shaderdesk/blob/main/LICENSE reads:
    "MIT License

     Copyright (c) 2026 Ilia Reingold

     Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy
     of this software and associated documentation files (the "Software"), to deal
     in the Software without restriction, ..."   (full text below)

  https://github.com/ilrein/shaderdesk/blob/main/README.md, section "License", reads:
    "MIT, see [LICENSE](LICENSE)."

  https://github.com/ilrein/shaderdesk/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md reads:
    "By contributing, you agree that your work is released under the [MIT License](LICENSE)."

Ported to Metal Shading Language for MacOS Live Wallpaper; see the note at the top of
main.metal for what was changed:

  - ShaderDesk's wrapper (scene_frame / scene_bake entry points, Uniforms, the shared bloom
    pass, present()) is removed; the picture is written by mainImage() with the original's
    own tone map and the sRGB curve from ShaderDesk's Scenes/Common.metal.
  - The baked cloud noise is replaced by clouds.png, an image generated for this port with a
    C copy of ShaderDesk's own tiling fBm (pgnoise / pfbm from Scenes/Common.metal, a 24 x 16
    cell tile; red = 6 octaves, green = 5 octaves, blue unused). It contains no third-party
    image data.
  - The scene clock is iTime; pow(x, 2.0) is written x * x.
  - The faulty neon signs flicker at 3 Hz instead of 12 Hz, to suit a desktop background.
  - The bloom pass is not reproduced.
  - hash11 / hash12 are copied from ShaderDesk's Scenes/Common.metal. They are David Hoskins'
    "Hash without Sine" (https://www.shadertoy.com/view/4djSRW), which carries the MIT notice
    reproduced in the second section below. ShaderDesk's copy does not credit him; the notice
    is restored here and in main.metal.

